course: world
lesson: 65
title: Glaciers, ice ages and polar lands
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A glacier is a huge mass of ice that slowly flows downhill under its own weight. It forms where more snow falls in winter than melts in summer, so year after year the snow piles up and is pressed into hard blue ice, the way a snowball squeezed in your hands turns icy.
explain: A glacier is a giant bulldozer and file: rocks frozen into its bottom scrape the land, so it carves deep valleys shaped like the letter U, digs hollows that later fill as lakes, and drops heaps of mixed rock and clay where it melts. The fjords of Norway are U-shaped valleys later flooded by the sea.
explain: An ice age is a long cold time when ice sheets spread over much of the land. The last cold period ended about 11,700 years ago; at its peak, ice up to 3 kilometres thick covered Canada and northern Europe, and so much water was locked in ice that the sea was about 120 metres lower than today.
explain: The polar lands are cold because sunlight reaches them at a low slant, and white ice reflects most of it back. Antarctica in the south is a continent of land under an ice sheet, with no people living there for good; the Arctic in the north is mostly an ocean covered by floating sea ice, ringed by lands where people such as the Inuit live.
explain: Ice sheets hold about two thirds of the Earth's fresh water. When ice on land melts, the sea rises; when floating sea ice melts, the sea hardly changes, just as melting ice cubes do not make a full glass overflow.
fact: A glacier is a large mass of ice that moves slowly downhill.
fact: Glaciers form where more snow falls than melts, year after year.
fact: Glaciers carve valleys shaped like the letter U; rivers cut valleys shaped like the letter V.
fact: A glacier can carry huge boulders far from where they broke off and drop them when the ice melts.
fact: A fjord is a deep U-shaped valley carved by a glacier and later flooded by the sea.
fact: The last ice age ended about 11,700 years ago.
fact: During the last ice age the sea level was about 120 metres lower than today.
fact: Antarctica is a continent of land covered by a thick ice sheet.
fact: The Arctic is mostly an ocean covered with floating sea ice.
fact: Polar bears live in the Arctic and penguins live in the south, mostly around Antarctica.
fact: White ice and snow reflect most of the sunlight that falls on them.
fact: Melting ice on land raises the sea level; melting floating sea ice hardly changes it.
fact: An iceberg is a piece of ice that has broken off a glacier into the sea, and most of it lies under the water.
exercise: What is a glacier? | A huge mass of ice moving slowly downhill | A frozen lake high in the mountains that never melts | A cloud of tiny ice crystals high in the sky | A mountain made of white rock that looks like ice
exercise: How does a glacier form? | More snow falls than melts, and it is pressed into ice over years | A whole river freezes solid in one very cold winter and stays frozen | Sea water is blown onto the land and freezes | Hail stones stick together in one storm
exercise: What shape of valley does a glacier carve? | A U shape | A V shape | A W shape | A narrow slit
exercise: What is a fjord? | A glacier valley flooded by the sea | A frozen waterfall high up in the mountains | A hill of sand left behind by the wind | A round lake in the top of an old volcano
exercise: When did the last ice age end? | About 11,700 years ago | About 100 years ago | About 66 million years ago | It is still at its coldest now
exercise: Why was the sea lower during the ice age? | Much of the water was locked up in ice on land | The oceans boiled away in the heat | The Moon pulled huge amounts of the water out into space | The rivers stopped carrying any water
exercise: Which is true about Antarctica? | It is a continent of land under thick ice | It is a frozen ocean with no land under it | It is where polar bears hunt seals | It has large cities with millions of people
exercise: Which is true about the Arctic? | It is mostly an ocean covered with sea ice | It is a continent covered by a thick ice sheet | It is where most penguins live | It is warm because it is near the Sun
exercise: Where do polar bears live? | In the Arctic | In Antarctica | In both polar lands | In the deserts
exercise: Why are polar lands so cold? | Sunlight comes at a low slant and white ice reflects much of it | They are the farthest places from the Sun | There is no air at the poles | The Earth's core is cold under them
exercise: What is an iceberg? | A piece of a glacier that has broken off into the sea | A mountain of snow on the land that slides down to the sea | A frozen wave in the ocean | A cloud of ice near the ground
exercise: How much of an iceberg is under the water? | Most of it | Almost none of it | Exactly half | It all floats on top
exercise: What do ice sheets hold? | About two thirds of the Earth's fresh water | Most of the Earth's salt water from the oceans | All of the water that runs in the Earth's rivers | Only the rain water that fell there last year
exercise: How does a glacier carve the land? | Rocks frozen in its base scrape the ground as it moves | Its cold water slowly dissolves the hard rock like sugar | It blows the soil away like the wind | It melts the rock with its weight
test: Walking in Scotland, you find a wide valley with a flat floor and steep sides like the letter U, but no ice. What most likely made it? | A glacier during the ice age | A small stream over a few years | An earthquake that split the land | People digging for coal
test: A huge boulder of granite sits alone in a field where all the rock below is limestone. How could it have got there? | A glacier carried it and dropped it when the ice melted | A river rolled it uphill | It grew there from the limestone | A volcano threw it there last year
test: All the floating ice in the Arctic Ocean melts one summer. What happens to the sea level? | It hardly changes, because the ice was already floating | It rises by many metres | It falls by many metres | The oceans overflow onto every continent
test: If the ice sheet of Greenland, which lies on land, melted, what would happen? | The sea would rise and flood low coasts | The sea would sink lower | Nothing, because the ice already floats | The sea would turn into fresh water
test: During the ice age, people walked from Asia to North America on dry land where there is sea today. How was that possible? | The sea was lower because so much water was frozen in ice sheets | The continents were joined by a giant bridge of ice built across the open sea | The ocean between them had boiled away | People could walk on the sea floor under water
test: A zoo shows a polar bear and a penguin side by side in an "Antarctic" display. What is wrong? | Polar bears live in the Arctic, not in Antarctica | Penguins live only in the Arctic, far away from the bears | Both animals live only in hot lands near the equator | Nothing, both live together on the ice in Antarctica
test: Snow falls on a high mountain each winter, but every summer all of it melts. Will a glacier form there? | No, because the snow must pile up year after year | Yes, because snow falls every winter | Yes, as long as the mountain is high | No, because glaciers form only in the sea
test: Why does dark sea water warm up faster than white ice when the Sun shines on both? | Dark water takes in more sunlight while white ice reflects it | Water is always closer to the Sun | Ice makes its own cold air | The Sun shines only on water
test: A ship's captain sees a small iceberg ahead. Why must he steer far around it? | Most of the iceberg is hidden under the water | Icebergs move much faster than ships can steer away | Icebergs give off a poisonous gas as they melt | Icebergs pull ships towards them like magnets
test: why are there no penguins up by the north pole, polar bears'd eat em? | Penguins live in the south, mostly around Antarctica | Penguins live at the North Pole but hide in winter | Polar bears ate all the penguins in the north long ago | The north is too warm for penguins to live there
test: how come norway's got those deep long sea valleys, the fjord things | Glaciers carved deep U-shaped valleys that the sea later flooded | Rivers cut deep V-shaped valleys right down to the sea | Earthquakes split the coast into long deep cracks | Waves wore away the soft rock over many years
test: Leo says if the arctic sea ice melts the sea'll rise loads. right? | No, melting floating sea ice hardly changes the sea level | Yes, any melting ice at all raises the level of the sea by a lot | Yes, but only the ice near the North Pole | No, melting sea ice makes the sea level fall
test: a video said Antarctica is just a big frozen ocean like the Arctic. true? | No, Antarctica is land covered by a thick ice sheet | Yes, both poles are frozen oceans with no land | Yes, but the Antarctic ice is thicker than the Arctic | No, Antarctica is land with almost no ice on it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a glacier? | A large mass of ice moving slowly downhill | A floating sheet of frozen sea water | A deep valley flooded by the sea | A lake which freezes each winter
exam: Identify the characteristic form of a valley carved by a glacier. | A valley shaped like the letter U | A valley shaped like the letter V | A valley shaped like the letter W | A valley of perfectly circular form
exam: It is given that ice upon land melts, and that floating sea ice likewise melts. Determine which of the two shall raise the level of the sea. | The melting of ice upon land | The melting of floating sea ice | Both shall raise it equally | Neither shall raise it in any degree
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the polar regions. | Antarctica is a continent of land beneath ice, whereas the Arctic is chiefly frozen ocean | The Arctic is a continent of land beneath ice, whereas Antarctica is chiefly frozen ocean | Both are continents of land beneath ice | Both are oceans covered with floating ice
